      "updated": 1794847739583,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why is Constructor injection needed \n=================================\n\nCar cannot exist without engine .\n\nPrinter mandatorily needs Ink Cartridge .\n\nSmartphone Mandatorily needs Battery .\n\nIt means that some dependencies are mandatory .\n\nAnd for these dependencies it always recommended to use Constructor\nInjection .\n\n# Setter Injection vs Constructor Injection\n\n## 1. Setter Injection\n\nIn Setter Injection, dependencies are injected into an object using setter\nmethods after the object has been created.\n\n**Example:**\n\n\npublic class Programmer {\n    private Computer cp;\n\n    public void setCp(Computer cp) {\n        this.cp = cp;\n    }\n}\n\n\nSpring XML:\n\nxml\n<bean id=\"prg\" class=\"in.spring.beans.Programmer\">\n    <property name=\"cp\" ref=\"ltp\"/>\n</bean>\n\nSpring internally performs:\n\njava\nProgrammer p = new Programmer();\np.setCp(laptop);\n\n\n### Disadvantages of Setter Injection\n\n1. **Incomplete Object:** An object can be created without its required\ndependencies.\n\n2. **NullPointerException:** If a required dependency is not injected and we\ntry to use it, NullPointerException may occur.\n\n3. **Mutable Object:** Dependencies can be changed even after object\ncreation by calling setter methods.\n\n4. **Optional Dependency:** Setter injection does not enforce that a\ndependency must be provided.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Constructor Injection\n\nIn Constructor Injection, dependencies are injected through the\nconstructor at the time of object creation.\n\n**Example:**\n\njava\npublic class Programmer {\n\n    private final Computer cp;\n\n    public Programmer(Computer cp) {\n        this.cp = cp;\n    }\n\n    public void writeCode() {\n        cp.start();\n    }\n}\n\n\nSpring XML:\n\nxml\n<bean id=\"ltp\" class=\"in.spring.beans.Laptop\"/>\n\n<bean id=\"prg\" class=\"in.spring.beans.Programmer\">\n    <constructor-arg ref=\"ltp\"/>\n</bean>\n\n\nSpring internally performs:\n\n\njava\nProgrammer p = new Programmer(laptop);\n\n\n### Advantages of Constructor Injection\n\n1. **Mandatory Dependencies:** Required dependencies must be provided\nduring object creation.\n\n2. **No Incomplete Object:** The object cannot be created without its\nrequired constructor arguments.\n\n3. **Immutability:** Using `final` fields prevents reassignment of\ndependencies after construction.\n\n4. **Better Testability:** Dependencies can easily be supplied while creating\nobjects in unit tests.\n\n5. **Early Error Detection:** Missing required dependencies can be detected\nduring object creation.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Difference Between Setter and Constructor Injection\n\n| Setter Injection | Constructor Injection |\n|---|---|\n| Dependency is injected after object creation. | Dependency is injected\nduring object creation. |\n| Uses setter methods. | Uses constructor. |\n| Dependencies can be optional. | Suitable for mandatory dependencies. |\n| Object can be created without dependencies. | Required dependencies must\nbe supplied. |\n| Supports changing dependencies later. | Supports immutable dependencies\nusing `final`. |\n| Best for optional dependencies. | Best for mandatory dependencies. |\n\n## 4. Why Is Constructor Injection Preferred?\n\nConstructor Injection is generally preferred because it ensures that an\nobject is created with all its required dependencies.\n\nIt prevents incomplete object creation, supports immutability, improves\ntestability, and makes dependencies explicit.\n\n**Interview Answer:**\n\n\"Constructor injection is generally preferred over setter injection for\nmandatory dependencies because it ensures that all required dependencies\nare provided at the time of object creation. It prevents incomplete object\ncreation, supports immutability through final fields, and improves testability.\nSetter injection is more suitable for optional dependencies.\"\n\n## 5. Important Point\n\n- Use **Constructor Injection** for mandatory dependencies.\n- Use **Setter Injection** for optional dependencies.\n\nBoth are valid Dependency Injection techniques in Spring.\n\n\n--> Constructor injection is a technique in which Spring provides the requires\ndependencies while creating the object by calling its constructor .\n\nSyntax \n---------\n\n<constructor-arg value = \" ... \"  index = \" ... \"  type = \" ... \" ref = \" ... \" />",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Why is Constructor injection needed \n=================================\n\nCar cannot exist without engine .\n\nPrinter mandatorily needs Ink Cartridge .\n\nSmartphone Mandatorily needs Battery .\n\nIt means that some dependencies are mandatory .\n\nAnd for these dependencies it always recommended to use Constructor Injection .\n\n# Setter Injection vs Constructor Injection\n\n## 1. Setter Injection\n\nIn Setter Injection, dependencies are injected into an object using setter methods after the object has been created.\n\n**Example:**\n\n\npublic class Programmer {\n    private Computer cp;\n\n    public void setCp(Computer cp) {\n        this.cp = cp;\n    }\n}\n\n\nSpring XML:\n\nxml\n<bean id=\"prg\" class=\"in.spring.beans.Programmer\">\n    <property name=\"cp\" ref=\"ltp\"/>\n</bean>\n\nSpring internally performs:\n\njava\nProgrammer p = new Programmer();\np.setCp(laptop);\n\n\n### Disadvantages of Setter Injection\n\n1. **Incomplete Object:** An object can be created without its required dependencies.\n\n2. **NullPointerException:** If a required dependency is not injected and we try to use it, NullPointerException may occur.\n\n3. **Mutable Object:** Dependencies can be changed even after object creation by calling setter methods.\n\n4. **Optional Dependency:** Setter injection does not enforce that a dependency must be provided.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Constructor Injection\n\nIn Constructor Injection, dependencies are injected through the constructor at the time of object creation.\n\n**Example:**\n\njava\npublic class Programmer {\n\n    private final Computer cp;\n\n    public Programmer(Computer cp) {\n        this.cp = cp;\n    }\n\n    public void writeCode() {\n        cp.start();\n    }\n}\n\n\nSpring XML:\n\nxml\n<bean id=\"ltp\" class=\"in.spring.beans.Laptop\"/>\n\n<bean id=\"prg\" class=\"in.spring.beans.Programmer\">\n    <constructor-arg ref=\"ltp\"/>\n</bean>\n\n\nSpring internally performs:\n\n\njava\nProgrammer p = new Programmer(laptop);\n\n\n### Advantages of Constructor Injection\n\n1. **Mandatory Dependencies:** Required dependencies must be provided during object creation.\n\n2. **No Incomplete Object:** The object cannot be created without its required constructor arguments.\n\n3. **Immutability:** Using `final` fields prevents reassignment of dependencies after construction.\n\n4. **Better Testability:** Dependencies can easily be supplied while creating objects in unit tests.\n\n5. **Early Error Detection:** Missing required dependencies can be detected during object creation.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Difference Between Setter and Constructor Injection\n\n| Setter Injection | Constructor Injection |\n|---|---|\n| Dependency is injected after object creation. | Dependency is injected during object creation. |\n| Uses setter methods. | Uses constructor. |\n| Dependencies can be optional. | Suitable for mandatory dependencies. |\n| Object can be created without dependencies. | Required dependencies must be supplied. |\n| Supports changing dependencies later. | Supports immutable dependencies using `final`. |\n| Best for optional dependencies. | Best for mandatory dependencies. |\n\n## 4. Why Is Constructor Injection Preferred?\n\nConstructor Injection is generally preferred because it ensures that an object is created with all its required dependencies.\n\nIt prevents incomplete object creation, supports immutability, improves testability, and makes dependencies explicit.\n\n**Interview Answer:**\n\n\"Constructor injection is generally preferred over setter injection for mandatory dependencies because it ensures that all required dependencies are provided at the time of object creation. It prevents incomplete object creation, supports immutability through final fields, and improves testability. Setter injection is more suitable for optional dependencies.\"\n\n## 5. Important Point\n\n- Use **Constructor Injection** for mandatory dependencies.\n- Use **Setter Injection** for optional dependencies.\n\nBoth are valid Dependency Injection techniques in Spring.\n\n\n--> Constructor injection is a technique in which Spring provides the requires dependencies while creating the object by calling its constructor .\n\nSyntax \n---------\n\n<constructor-arg value = \" ... \"  index = \" ... \"  type = \" ... \" ref = \" ... \" />",
